securities and exchange commission chair  gary gensler has called cryptocurrency  the wild west regulators have cracked  down for years on individual token  issuers but so far the sec has not taken  any major enforcement actions against  the big crypto exchanges the commission  has made waves in one area though crypto  lending  we've seen the sec and six state  attorneys general threaten lawsuits and  shutdowns for companies offering lending  so this begs the question what is crypto  lending and why is it the one part of  the crypto universe where we've seen  regulators publicly threaten action  against the big names here's how what  happens with crypto lending could affect  the entire crypto market  let's start with the basics crypto  lending is exactly what it sounds like  investors who hold cryptocurrencies like  bitcoin or ethereum and even many stable  coins like usdc can lend their holdings  of coins to other investors through  lending platforms investors can do a few  things to start they can use crypto as  collateral for their own loans they can  also lend their crypto to other traders  who might want to use it to lever up  their positions to make bigger bets or  speculators might look to borrow crypto  to take out short positions betting the  prices will fall it's actually pretty  similar to old-fashioned banking with  crypto platforms like celsius blockfi  and others playing the role of bankers  users lend the platform their crypto and  the platform lends it to other users or  traders or platforms in exchange for a  fee  then the platform passes some of that  profit back to users in the form of  interest  since crypto is a new asset class and  one that lacks a lot of actual cash  traders and exchanges who want to borrow  in the space are willing to pay a high  price in order to do so but why would  anyone want to lend their crypto  the answer quite simply is it pays  very well  lending can be an attractive proposition  for crypto hodlers excuse me crypto  holders with diamond hands who don't  want to sell before prices go to the  moon but also want to earn some stable  passive income and the yields are pretty  nice one platform is offering up to 14  interest on some stable coins and up to  8.5 interest on bitcoin compare that to  the  0.06 percent interest the average u.s  bank savings account pays and you can  see why there's a lot of interest  nobody wants to have cash sitting in a  bank account earning zero percent that's  jeff doorman chief investment officer of  arca investments his firm manages 700  million dollars in digital assets and  he's both a lender and a borrower in the  crypto space well the same thing is now  true in digital assets if you own it and  you're not doing anything with it  there's a variety of different ways to  now earn a yield by staking it by  participating in an ecosystem or by  lending it to somebody else who's going  to do those things instead of you and  you can earn a return for lending it to  them and as long as you think you can  make enough in your trading volume to  offset the amount of interest that  you're paying you're willing to do so  but  and there's always a but  crypto lending isn't just a matter of  watching your gains stack up there are  some real risks  first unlike a traditional savings  account your deposits are not insured by  the federal deposit insurance  corporation or anyone else that means if  your crypto goes missing or if it gets  stolen in a hack  or if the company just doesn't want to  give it back to you well that's too bad  and we've already seen it happen in  august hackers stole 600 million dollars  worth of crypto from finance platform  poly network and at thodex the crypto  exchange's founder and ceo allegedly  fled with about 2 billion worth of funds  those are just a couple examples the  second potential problem is that much  the crypto market is backed by other  cryptocurrencies and stable coins rather  than cash  that can mean reduced liquidity you  could find yourself waiting a lot longer  to get your crypto back  and there's the potential for users  coins to get stuck during times of  market distress or extreme volatility  this is especially important in a market  like crypto where 20 daily price swings  come with the territory stable coins are  meant to be stable but they aren't  always and in fact no one's quite sure  what the backing assets are behind some  of the biggest stable coins this has  made regulators worry that in a pinch  crypto users could see their money  disappear so you can see there are some  potential dangers with lending and  borrowing crypto but why is it that  regulators have stepped in on lending  but held back on restrictions for crypto  trading at big exchanges to explain that  i spoke with my colleague alexander  osipovic who covers exchanges and market  structure for the journal  many of the firms that are doing uh  cryptocurrency lending and borrowing  right now they have the viewpoint that  they don't need to register with sec  they're not doing anything that touches  on u.s securities laws obviously the  regulators disagree with that i guess  the main concern from the regulator's  standpoint is that there's really little  disclosure about what's actually  happening behind the scenes with these  companies the regulators basically don't  want crypto to grow into a giant asset  class in its own right without any sort  of regulatory framework around it they  want to impose disclosure requirements  on different crypto firms they want the  police for fraud and they want to make  sure that they have a handle on this  asset class before it gets so big that  it might become a source of systemic  financial risk the sec reportedly sent a  notice to coinbase in september warning  that they were planning to take action  against the company for its crypto  lending product coinbase says the sec  pointed to the howie test a 1946 rubric  that seeks to determine whether or not  something is a security you may not have  heard of it but the howie test is really  important in the world of financial  regulation it asks three questions  one  is there an investment of money two  is there a common enterprise meaning  will customers assets be mixed in with  other customers assets to fund the same  venture or project and three is there a  reasonable expectation of profit from  the efforts of others  legal experts seem to agree that all  three apply to crypto lending this is  important because if crypto lending  qualifies as a security then the sec has  the right to regulate it or even shut it  down the commission has not yet  announced any major actions related to  buying and selling crypto against big  exchanges like coinbase binance and ftx  which ran ads during the world series as  the official crypto exchange of major  league baseball without the sec stepping  in with enforcement actions most crypto  exchanges have largely just assumed they  have the green light to do as they  please in fact despite the actions taken  by the sec and the six states most  crypto lenders are still operating as  well while coinbase shut down its  planned crypto lending option blockfi  celsius and others are still in business  they're awaiting rulings on orders  brought against them by the various  attorneys general so what does all this  mean for crypto investors and for the  crypto market as a whole crypto lending  is different than crypto trading because  most crypto holders aren't earning  interest on their coins however  decisions by the states on crypto  lending could be seen as setting a  precedent for the broader industry if  regulators are going to take a position  on lending they could also be close to  taking a position on crypto trading and  that could mean regulations are coming  for dogecoin litecoin stellar polka dot  chain link hell the whole market  so don't sleep  if you enjoyed this video make sure and  hit the like button and if you're  interested in markets 